WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.960
Cloud security is the practice of protecting cloud-hosted systems, data, identities,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.060
networks, services, and workloads.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.000
It is not one product, one setting,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.740
or one team. It is an operating model for using elastic,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.480
API-driven infrastructure without losing control of access, exposure, data handling,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.860
and operational visibility.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.400
Cloud environments feel different from traditional on-premises environments because change

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.780
is faster and more programmable.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.680
An engineer can create a resource, attach storage, grant a role, expose a service,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.940
or deploy a workload through an API, a pipeline, or an infrastructure as code change.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.080
That speed is useful, but it also means mistakes can scale quickly.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.500
A permissive policy can apply to many resources. A public storage bucket can expose more

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.400
data than intended.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.860
A default network rule can make a service reachable before anyone has reviewed whether it

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:58.880
should be.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.780
The core domains are identity, network, data, workloads, logging, configuration,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.200
and governance.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.940
Identity controls who and what can use the cloud APIs. Network controls decide what is

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.240
reachable and from where.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.540
Data controls protect storage, databases, backups, keys,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.200
and retention. Workload controls cover virtual machines, containers, functions, images,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.820
secrets, and runtime behavior.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.800
Logging and monitoring help teams understand who did what, from where, to which resource,

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.140
and when.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.280
Configuration and governance make secure behavior repeatable across accounts,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.060
subscriptions, projects, and teams.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.520
A practical cloud security routine begins with inventory. What exists?

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.140
Who owns it? What data does it handle?

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.880
Which identities can change it? Is it public, private,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.260
or reachable through a controlled entry point?

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.760
What logs are enabled? What baseline should this resource follow?

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.620
These questions sound simple, but they are the foundation for every meaningful control.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:09.240
Cloud security also requires shared accountability. Developers, engineers, administrators,

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.020
analysts, managers, and governance teams all touch the risk picture.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.760
The goal is not to slow every change to a crawl. The goal is to make secure defaults,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.860
least privilege, review, monitoring, and remediation part of normal engineering work.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.280
When teams know their assets, limit access, protect data, monitor activity,

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.980
and review configuration continuously,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.920
cloud security becomes a repeatable way of operating rather than a last-minute checklist.
